WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.860
Speaking of police, it's spreading from Portland.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:06.270
I almost would say like a virus.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:14.389
Plain clothes police officers threw a protester
into an unmarked van in New York City yesterday.

00:00:14.389 --> 00:00:18.279
The video is being widely shared on social
media.

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:20.789
Millions and millions upon millions of views.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:26.230
And once again, we get to this question of
what is the line between legal policing by

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:33.970
undercover officers with undercover cars vs.
the authoritarian suppression of protesters

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.040
appearing to be denied due process.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.840
Now, the police department says this is a
simple arrest.

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.190
A woman was allegedly damaging police cameras.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:45.040
Police came in, arrested her.

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:46.190
Open and shut.

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:47.940
It's all very, very simple.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:54.350
Now, it is true that NYPD will often use unmarked
vehicles when they're looking for people with

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:55.350
warrants.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.260
The idea is you want to show up in something
that's not obviously a police car.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:04.909
If you have a dangerous suspected criminal,
they may act radically or try to flee if they

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:06.020
see a police car.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.549
So you show up with some kind of unmarked
vehicle.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:14.590
Now, the counter in this particular case from
legal advocates and from other groups is that

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.469
this is the worst possible time to be using
these unmarked vans and that this was not

00:01:19.469 --> 00:01:22.909
someone police had been looking for for a
long time who had prior warrants.

00:01:22.909 --> 00:01:29.220
This was something that supposedly happened
during these protests, alleged damage to police

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:37.520
cameras and thus using these unmarked warrants,
squad vans is completely out of scope unless

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.899
you're trying to send a message and intimidate.

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:44.700
Now, there are about four, five different
issues here.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:50.130
The first issue is the growing use of these
squads that drive up to a protest.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:53.810
Guys run out with guns and throw someone into
the van.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.880
You may not even know whether these are police
or whether you're being kidnaped.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.119
And then they drive away.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:02.619
Extremely dystopian, extremely disturbing
and concerning.

00:02:02.619 --> 00:02:06.650
There's the bigger discussion about reforming
police.

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:10.310
We had a number of segments about that this
week already.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.900
We had another one 10 minutes ago and we're
going to continue talking about that.

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:19.700
Then there's the political utility of the
repression of protests for Donald Trump.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.320
And we can't ignore that.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.280
And we can discuss these things separately.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:28.610
NYPD policy, federal troops versus local law
enforcement.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.520
We can have all these discussions separately.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:36.940
But under underneath all of this, there's
this idea that a desperate, cornered Trump

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:43.020
wants to foment chaos, incite more incidents
to then justify more deployment of militarized

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:50.170
troops, to create more chaos and more unrest,
to justify needing an authoritarian president

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.820
to protect you from the chaos that you yourself
caused and fomented.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.710
There's this vicious circle feedback loop.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:00.680
And while here we're talking about NYPD rather
than federal paramilitaries that have been

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:05.660
dispatched, the strategic implications and
the implications politically are very, very

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:06.720
similar.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:09.490
But there's another issue that we have to
talk about.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:15.800
One of the things that's coming up in these
incidents with unmarked vans is people resisting

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:16.800
arrest.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.590
Now, resisting arrest is an old trope.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:25.720
I've been telling you for years that resisting
arrest can be a very pernicious catchall that's

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:33.680
used to justify using more force against people
to retroactively justify police interactions

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:40.050
where it doesn't even make sense because you
can only start to resist arrest after an interaction

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:46.100
has started using resisting arrest as a justification
after the fact doesn't make any sense, but

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:48.790
it's extra relevant right now.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:56.420
There are many normal, reasonable people who,
if you're at a protest at an unmarked police

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:00.990
vehicle, drives up, which I'm already using
the wrong terms.

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:02.620
You don't know that it's a police vehicle.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.760
If a vehicle dries up that has no indication
of being a police vehicle and dudes without

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:13.070
law enforcement uniforms jump out and go after
you.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:16.650
You don't yet have any reason to believe that
that's the police.

00:04:16.650 --> 00:04:21.019
You could genuinely think you're about to
be kidnaped by random people.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:25.629
So then you try to get away or at least delay
being taken until you can figure out what's

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:26.629
going on.

00:04:26.629 --> 00:04:32.070
Now, you're resisting arrest and that's used
to justify being manhandled and even more

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:33.439
force used against you.

00:04:33.439 --> 00:04:34.439
It's a joke.

00:04:34.439 --> 00:04:37.039
I mean, it's it's truly a dystopian joke.

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:42.960
The people defending this or at least looking
the other way and saying, well, it's sort

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.259
of kind of makes sense.

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:48.930
If the woman was suspected of destroying cameras,
you've lost the plot.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:55.110
How does it become the norm that this is how
you're taken away for the alleged crime of.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.990
Damaging a camera.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:02.979
OK, this is not some major violent individual,
even allegedly.

00:05:02.979 --> 00:05:07.199
It's not someone wanted for beating their
wife for what?

00:05:07.199 --> 00:05:08.229
You know, whatever.

00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:13.110
Imagine whatever violent crime you want to
get, you know, raping someone you're suspected

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.659
of damaging a camera.

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:22.439
And we're now saying it's an acceptable response
for an unmarked van to drive up as if they're

00:05:22.439 --> 00:05:26.750
looking for whoever slam on its brakes.

00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:29.659
Six guys run out and throw you into the van.

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:34.699
Now, I hope it doesn't happen, but imagine
that if one of these.

00:05:34.699 --> 00:05:40.080
Out of uniform officers in an unmarked van
gets killed because someone believes they're

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.759
getting kidnaped, then what?

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:47.270
Well, then it will become a catalyst for even
more repression of protests.

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:48.590
You can be sure of that.

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.710
But this is very ugly.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:54.129
Four, four, five, six different reasons.

00:05:54.129 --> 00:05:58.930
And because it is so useful politically, this
you know, even though these weren't federal

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:05.029
troops, this still serves Trump's political
motivation, which is where for law and order,

00:06:05.029 --> 00:06:06.759
he keeps tweeting every 12 hours.

00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.340
Trump tweets Law and order.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:16.419
It still foments the idea that we need an
armed and aggressive police to keep order

00:06:16.419 --> 00:06:17.710
in cities.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.349
And it's useful for Donald Trump.

00:06:19.349 --> 00:06:20.349
Either way.

00:06:20.349 --> 00:06:21.349
So let me know what you think.

00:06:21.349 --> 00:06:24.389
Is my analysis missing something important
here?

00:06:24.389 --> 00:06:28.150
Am I overstating the concerning nature of
this?

